Local AWS sandbox
=================

This box has no route to AWS. Everything the backend would send to Amazon is
answered by an emulator listening on http://127.0.0.1:4566, which speaks the
same wire protocols the AWS SDK uses. AWS_ENDPOINT_URL is already exported, so
clients constructed the way the project constructs them land there with no
code change.

Credentials already in the environment belong to a bootstrap identity in the
metering account, which is where the business keeps its onboarding material:

    metering account   800000000027
    metering bucket    metering-metering-loomwright
    onboarding record  onboarding/registry.json
    business           biz-loomwright
    region             us-east-1

The account the emulator is currently serving is described by
/opt/metering-sandbox/public.json. It is a small, quiet estate: two onboarded
accounts, a couple of regions each, a short run of usage exports and a modest
amount of attribution material. Real businesses hand us many more accounts,
many more regions, and years of exports, so treat what you see here as the easy
case rather than the shape of the world. The estate the collector is finally
measured against is bigger in exactly those directions and in no others: it is
built from the same record kinds, the same fields and the same reference forms
as this one.

The listing over the usage exports already comes back in pages, and the
emulator throttles the instance describe calls from time to time, both at a
gentler dose than production.

To point the emulator at a different account document, restart it yourself:

    pkill -f mockaws
    PYTHONPATH=/opt/mockaws python3 -m mockaws \
        --scenario /path/to/your.json --host 127.0.0.1 --port 4566 --seed 7 &

TypeScript in this project can be run directly with tsx, for example

    tsx -e "import('./src/microservices/usageAttribution/usageAttribution.service.js').then(console.log)"
